+
+**Read-only recovery selectors.** `recover(ExecutionId)` takes the `SHARED-READ` lease and
+reads **one** consistent committed ledger snapshot carrying the receipt, the private
+recovery association **and** the `AttemptCustodyV1` phase together. Terminality is decided
+inside that single snapshot and never from a separately timed liveness probe or an in-memory
+active set: receipt absent with phase `settled` is the failed answer; phase `admitted` is
+`unknown-attempt-open`; no attempt row is `unknown-attempt-unobserved`. Carrier observations
+(journal tail, witness, SC-TRUST floor) are captured with before/after stability brackets and
+at most one fresh journal retry. It performs no witness INIT/REVERT/ADVANCE, no high-water
+raise, no store-binding allocation, no new execution grant, no fence acquisition and no wait
+on a writer. Security §S6 and §S9 own the exact carrier rules; the bounded algorithm is
+`architecture/commit-recovery-readonly.v2.md`.
+
+**Narrow assurance limit, stated here because recovery is often read as proof.** Confirming a
+historical commit from the retained carrier establishes that the sealing record is present
+with its joined digest, operation reference and Run identity, that the sequence is
+contiguous, and that no rollback below the last **observed** operation boundary occurred. It
+does **not** cryptographically authenticate the interior record prefix: the inherited chain
+value binds only the immediately previous body digest and index, and the witness names the
+tail body digest, so an interior substitution is not detected by either. The conclusion class
+is therefore `confirmed-under-retained-custody`, never a cryptographic proof, and an unmet
+anchor is reported `unknown`, never as invalidation of an earlier committed receipt.
 An acknowledged Run with missing/corrupt objects becomes partially available or
